<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamluk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mamluk was a soldier of slave origin, usually Turkic or Caucasian, bought as a boy and trained for military service in Islamic states, and often freed when his training ended. Mamluks became central to the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>. In some cases they seized power themselves, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Mamluk dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk.jpg" alt="Mamluk" width="290" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Hopfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Mamelukes_with_lances_on_horseback.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shadow caliphate continued in Cairo under <b>Mamluk</b> patronage until 1517, when the Ottoman Sultan Selim I's conquest of Egypt brought even that ceremonial institution to an end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Regions named in 1286, when <b>Mamluk</b> emissaries went to several rulers of central Sudan, include al-Anag, Ari, Bara, Befal, Danfou, Kedru, Kersa and Taka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ya’qub later defeated a <b>Mamluk</b> army near Urfa in 1480, and in 1488 his forces, together with those of the Shirvanshah, killed the militant Safavid leader Shaykh Haydar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Armeno-Mongol alliance Het'um I forged in 1247–1253 made Cilicia a partner in Hulagu's conquest of Syria and in Ghazan's campaigns of 1299–1303, and a target of the <b>Mamluks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It extinguished most of what remained, although Hama survived as a <b>Mamluk</b> vassal into the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Circassia was the homeland of the Adyghe peoples, a stateless country of princes and free communities that supplied Egypt with <b>Mamluk</b> sultans, resisted Russian expansion for a century, and was destroyed in 1864 by conquest and mass deportation, creating a diaspora that remains across the Middle East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
